WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:04.150
Hello and welcome back to another video
with ItsDrDan, and today we're going to be

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.840
going into drawing cis and trans alkenes.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:12.369
Now one of the things that's really common
to be asked of you by your professors

00:00:12.729 --> 00:00:15.719
is to draw structures in different ways.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.650
Mainly it's through expanded or complete
structures, condensed structures

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:24.540
or formulas, and line structures,
whether they're called geometric,

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.590
skeletal, there's many different names.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.950
Now, when you're doing it for alkenes,
it's kind of similar to drawing

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:35.690
cyclic structures where you need to
actually show the different shapes

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:40.429
of these bonds, especially when
you have cis or trans capabilities.

00:00:40.715 --> 00:00:42.955
You want to make sure you
label your molecules correctly.

00:00:43.435 --> 00:00:47.025
Now, what makes alkene so special
is the double bond is considered

00:00:47.025 --> 00:00:51.225
to be rigid, meaning that you can't
just rotate it however you want.

00:00:51.255 --> 00:00:54.925
It's kind of stuck in its exact structure.

00:00:55.585 --> 00:00:58.424
So when we're drawing these,
it's important that we

00:00:58.425 --> 00:00:59.624
show the bond connectivity.

00:01:00.615 --> 00:01:05.675
as well as that 3D visualization
when we're drawing these.

00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:14.065
So if I already asked you a question
of how to draw these, and then

00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:17.385
we'll go through this obviously as
an example, is how would you draw

00:01:17.525 --> 00:01:23.285
three trans hexene As expanded,
condensed, and as a line structure.

00:01:23.785 --> 00:01:24.905
So how do you go through it?

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:28.075
Well, the first thing is when it
comes to expanded, it's when you're

00:01:28.085 --> 00:01:31.875
drawing all the bonds, you're drawing
even all your hydrogens, and it tends

00:01:31.875 --> 00:01:33.695
to be quite a little bit of a mess.

00:01:34.455 --> 00:01:37.804
The biggest thing is when you're
trying to actually showcase these as

00:01:37.815 --> 00:01:42.655
cis and trans, is you need to give
indications that you actually have

00:01:43.225 --> 00:01:45.585
that trans capability in a structure.

00:01:45.585 --> 00:01:51.255
So what I like to do personally, so
with starting with expanded, is I

00:01:51.265 --> 00:01:55.945
start from the double bond and then
I draw my weight outwards from it.

00:01:56.005 --> 00:01:57.745
And I do the same thing with condensed.

00:01:58.304 --> 00:02:02.085
So for example, if I were to write
this, right, I have my double bond and

00:02:02.094 --> 00:02:07.244
being that it's at three transhexene,
I'm going to number my double bond

00:02:07.285 --> 00:02:11.105
as three and four for the carbons,
because remember when we're numbering,

00:02:11.665 --> 00:02:14.025
the first number is shown in the name.

00:02:14.555 --> 00:02:18.455
Now what we're going to do is we're
going to build this molecule outwards.

00:02:19.125 --> 00:02:24.384
So typically when we're drawing this,
we actually end up showing some of

00:02:24.385 --> 00:02:26.535
that three dimensional shape to it.

00:02:26.535 --> 00:02:31.395
It doesn't entirely showcase
only the 90 degree that you're

00:02:31.405 --> 00:02:32.774
kind of used to with these.

00:02:33.364 --> 00:02:37.065
We need to actually show like the angles
to kind of give it the appropriate look.

00:02:37.725 --> 00:02:41.685
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to
first start by drawing in all my carbons.

00:02:41.695 --> 00:02:43.995
So we have carbon number
five and number six.

00:02:44.804 --> 00:02:48.214
And then I'm going to put in
carbon number two and number one.

00:02:48.894 --> 00:02:51.774
Now what we'll do from there is
we're going to fill in all of our

00:02:51.774 --> 00:02:53.684
hydrogens, so keeping our counts.

00:02:54.394 --> 00:02:59.264
Um, here I'll put the numbers in so we can
prove to you that we still have a hexene.

00:02:59.755 --> 00:03:04.924
Notice that the carbons are moving in
opposite directions too, so we still have

00:03:04.924 --> 00:03:08.864
that kind of that trans look to it that
I'm trying to highlight here in yellow,

00:03:08.864 --> 00:03:13.094
which I think I'll change to red, just to
kind of help that contrast a little bit.

00:03:13.094 --> 00:03:13.204
Okay.

00:03:13.885 --> 00:03:16.265
Now what we're going to do is we're
going to fill our hydrogens in.

00:03:16.645 --> 00:03:19.595
So the first one, right,
we need to make four bonds.

00:03:19.985 --> 00:03:21.615
So we got to have three hydrogens.

00:03:22.154 --> 00:03:24.484
On the second one, we're going
to be the same kind of idea.

00:03:24.484 --> 00:03:27.784
We're going to try to keep that
90 degrees the best that we can.

00:03:28.194 --> 00:03:31.074
Um, even though it looks going
to look a little bit funny here.

00:03:31.075 --> 00:03:32.785
So we'll have one hydrogen, two hydrogens.

00:03:34.235 --> 00:03:38.025
On the next one, we have
one hydrogen, one hydrogen.

00:03:38.035 --> 00:03:41.234
And notice that I kind of put them
pointing in opposite directions to help

00:03:41.445 --> 00:03:43.545
illustrate that trans a little bit more.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.429
If you want to do them
as diagonals, you can.

00:03:46.489 --> 00:03:48.489
I just, I think that's completely fine.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.890
Um, it doesn't have to be perfect
when it comes to drawing every single

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:57.419
one of these, because it's not the
idea of it to show, uh, perfect

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.790
geometry when it comes expanded.

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:03.230
It's just to help us illustrate,
do we have that expanded look?

00:04:04.109 --> 00:04:05.770
Now, is this the only way you can do this?

00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:11.610
If you want to stick to, let's say, pure
90 degrees, you can do that as well.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:16.719
The one thing that I, also another way
of doing, one way that I learned, is

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:21.999
if you want to show trans, the whole
idea is you want to try to put the

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.930
different groups in opposite directions.

00:04:24.409 --> 00:04:27.819
So like, I'll have one hydrogen pointing
up, I'll have the other one pointing down.

00:04:28.445 --> 00:04:32.035
And being that those are opposite
of each other, I can then continue

00:04:32.065 --> 00:04:35.825
my branch as well, just like this.

00:04:35.885 --> 00:04:39.714
This is another option, which I think
is really good, because then you can

00:04:39.714 --> 00:04:41.824
actually draw everything as straight.

00:04:42.545 --> 00:04:46.455
So these are both acceptable
ways to do expanded structures.

00:04:46.895 --> 00:04:49.474
The most important thing is
that you're showing ways to

00:04:49.474 --> 00:04:51.995
show that trans capability.

00:04:51.995 --> 00:04:55.674
So notice that I have my hydrogens
on opposite sides, so both of

00:04:55.705 --> 00:04:57.804
these are acceptable ways to do it.

00:04:58.219 --> 00:05:00.599
It kind of just depends
on which one you prefer.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.610
So, now with condensed, the
whole idea here is, is we're

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.560
trying to simplify our structure.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.519
So let's look at that and how to do that.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.680
My button on every single carbon.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:12.319
So I do the same thing.

00:05:12.329 --> 00:05:13.479
I start at my double bond.

00:05:13.539 --> 00:05:14.979
I write my CHs in there.

00:05:15.399 --> 00:05:19.280
Now, when you are doing this, the
most important thing is we do want

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.800
to try to show that trans capability.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.130
So you could just write CHs.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:27.630
But personally, this
is where I like to, um,

00:05:33.535 --> 00:05:35.785
So now we're going to be showing
our condensed structures.

00:05:36.305 --> 00:05:39.525
What I personally like to do is
I like to do the same strategy.

00:05:39.565 --> 00:05:41.125
I like to start from the double bond.

00:05:41.545 --> 00:05:44.165
And what we're going to be doing
is we're actually going to be doing

00:05:44.165 --> 00:05:45.945
some three dimensional shape here.

00:05:46.445 --> 00:05:50.504
So I have one hydrogen pointing up and
one pointing down, and then I'm going

00:05:50.504 --> 00:05:52.904
to do my two carbons on each side.

00:05:53.375 --> 00:05:59.265
So when we have this trans structure,
You're going to write CH2CH3 being

00:05:59.265 --> 00:06:04.055
that we're minimizing each of these
different carbon areas that we have over

00:06:04.055 --> 00:06:07.285
here, bringing in all those hydrogens.

00:06:07.305 --> 00:06:10.324
And we're going to do the same
thing up here, um, as well.

00:06:10.635 --> 00:06:13.905
And notice I didn't draw it
pointing up and down the exact

00:06:13.905 --> 00:06:15.585
same way like I did the other one.

00:06:15.995 --> 00:06:18.895
That's okay, it does not have
to be the same way, because if I

00:06:18.895 --> 00:06:22.485
were to rotate this molecule, they
would be the exact same thing.

00:06:23.005 --> 00:06:25.115
So this is how you can
do a condensed structure.

00:06:25.655 --> 00:06:30.565
Now when it comes to trying to draw a
skeletal, we're going to be, I like to

00:06:30.565 --> 00:06:32.415
do where I started the double bond again.

00:06:32.455 --> 00:06:36.385
I think it makes it a little bit easier
because you're trying to make sure

00:06:36.385 --> 00:06:40.904
that you show how many carbons there
are, and that you have the correct

00:06:40.955 --> 00:06:43.925
geometry in your structure as well.

00:06:44.395 --> 00:06:51.609
So if you kind of look here, I have that
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, And I still have that,

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:56.909
that trans type of structure where they're
both pointing up and down in my molecule.

00:06:57.219 --> 00:06:59.549
Okay, which is kind of the
ultimate idea, see how they're

00:06:59.549 --> 00:07:00.830
both opposite of each other.

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:06.640
Which is that most important part when
we're trying to illustrate cis and trans.

00:07:07.169 --> 00:07:09.780
So this would be how to draw
skeletal, and you need to show

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:10.950
that in all your structures.

00:07:11.549 --> 00:07:16.520
If it was cis, let's say, instead, you
would all, which we're going to do in the

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:20.659
next one, you need to just make sure that
you have that U shape in your structure.

00:07:20.849 --> 00:07:23.140
Let's go on to our next
example and take a look.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.590
Okay, so for this example, we're
going to draw cis 5 t butyl 2

00:07:28.679 --> 00:07:33.620
octene as an expanded, condensed,
and also as a line structure.

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:36.790
So this one is a harder example that
I thought would be good to pick,

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:40.410
being that I feel like a lot of
tutorials and And the textbook examples

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:41.740
always go over really easy ones.

00:07:42.305 --> 00:07:45.385
So with this one, what we're going
to do is, I like to personally

00:07:45.385 --> 00:07:47.405
start with the double bond itself.

00:07:47.945 --> 00:07:50.544
Now when it comes to expanded,
the entire idea here is we're

00:07:50.545 --> 00:07:52.844
trying to show all of the bonds.

00:07:53.275 --> 00:07:55.205
So everything that's possible.

00:07:55.594 --> 00:07:59.184
And the thing was with this one as well,
it's also a really big thing that you

00:07:59.184 --> 00:08:03.335
try to use 90 degree angles as much as
possible, it's supposed to represent

00:08:03.345 --> 00:08:06.064
a Lewis structure is the entire idea.

00:08:06.445 --> 00:08:10.135
So I start with the double bond, which
was number two is where that bond starts.

00:08:10.785 --> 00:08:13.945
And what we're going to do is we're
going to fill in the rest of the chain.

00:08:14.435 --> 00:08:19.495
Now being that it is cis, what
we're going to see is here is it's

00:08:19.545 --> 00:08:25.535
easier if you emphasize carbon
bonds going in the same direction.

00:08:25.805 --> 00:08:29.285
So see how they're both going
horizontal, this is an option to do cis.

00:08:29.565 --> 00:08:33.705
It can also go together vertically
as well, which is another choice.

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:37.750
Right, so you're going to see that
I have my, my carbons going together

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:42.220
sideways, and then notice that I'm
putting my hydrogens together as well.

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:47.870
So this is helping us show that CIS
characteristic, right, and that's kind

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:52.419
of the goal with this is you need to
try to show it, um, even though this

00:08:52.429 --> 00:08:57.310
is not the most common way to draw
these kinds of structures, um, with

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:01.280
expanded, it's more to help you be
able to visualize all those bonds.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:01.299
Okay.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:03.430
So we're going to fill
in our carbons, right?

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:05.950
We're going to count all of our
bonds, and then we're going to

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:08.290
connect it to each individual carbon.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:13.060
So one of the easy ways to do this is draw
your carbon chain first, and then we're

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.369
going to fill in all those hydrogens.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:19.810
Now keep in mind, we do have a
special branch at number five, which

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:22.140
is one of, it's a t butyl branch.

00:09:22.569 --> 00:09:24.760
And if you're not sure exactly
about those, you can refer to

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:28.829
my video on different types of
butyl and propyl branches, which

00:09:28.829 --> 00:09:30.370
will help go over that as well.

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:32.860
But on number five we
have this butyl group.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:36.350
So what you can do is you can
actually draw like an awkwardly

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.270
long carbon bond so that it gives
you room to expand the structure.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:44.829
So we're going to start with the carbon,
right, and being this is four carbons,

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:49.830
we're going to draw three lines all
around this with a carbon at each one.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:53.000
And then you can see in yellow
all four carbons that are here.

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:56.150
Now what we'll do is next
we'll fill in our hydrogens.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.400
So we'll have three hydrogens
on each one of these.

00:09:59.845 --> 00:10:02.575
Little methyl attachments in the t butyl.

00:10:03.075 --> 00:10:08.615
So you can see we have our complete
structure here and it's every little

00:10:08.615 --> 00:10:12.035
detail that we can do, and this is
not the only way you can do expanded.

00:10:12.035 --> 00:10:13.564
There's other options too.

00:10:13.875 --> 00:10:16.714
It's kind of all about your
interpretation of how to show it

00:10:16.714 --> 00:10:18.015
and what's accepted in your class.

00:10:18.025 --> 00:10:19.655
But this is one way to do it.

00:10:20.325 --> 00:10:24.165
Now, the next part with condensed, this
is when you start getting a little easier.

00:10:24.475 --> 00:10:27.785
Um, being that now you can actually
sort of show some of those three

00:10:27.785 --> 00:10:29.105
dimensional characteristics.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.220
So let's say I start once
again on the double bond.

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:37.490
What you can do is now is you can
actually use some diagonal lines.

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:40.730
So I have my CH3 from carbon number one.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:44.549
I'm going to point it upwards
to show that U bowl shape.

00:10:44.999 --> 00:10:47.779
So we have number one, number
two, number three, and then

00:10:47.779 --> 00:10:49.410
we'll have number four coming up.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:53.280
So we can see that, okay,
well, here's our CH2.

00:10:53.775 --> 00:10:56.515
And we have that bowl shape.

00:10:56.515 --> 00:11:00.775
So I'm going to highlight that in green
just so you can kind of see that U

00:11:00.775 --> 00:11:02.804
shape that, that is, that's here, right?

00:11:02.814 --> 00:11:04.605
That's going to be the cis part.

00:11:04.615 --> 00:11:06.425
So that's exactly what we want.

00:11:06.985 --> 00:11:08.455
Now we're going to continue that.

00:11:08.855 --> 00:11:11.275
So we're going to go to the next
one, which is carbon number five.

00:11:11.745 --> 00:11:15.375
We have carbon six and
seven, which will condense.

00:11:16.165 --> 00:11:19.224
Um, so let's kind of
label those for ourselves.

00:11:20.445 --> 00:11:23.385
And now with our t butyl, this
is where it kind of is up to

00:11:23.385 --> 00:11:24.525
you, how you want to do it.

00:11:24.974 --> 00:11:28.265
Personally, I like doing a little
line to have it come above.

00:11:28.574 --> 00:11:31.295
You can also put it in
parentheses as well.

00:11:31.685 --> 00:11:34.825
So it's going to be one carbon
with a CH3, you're gonna have

00:11:34.825 --> 00:11:37.065
three CH3s coming all around it.

00:11:37.384 --> 00:11:39.964
And that's what we saw in
this structure up here, right?

00:11:39.964 --> 00:11:44.145
You see like this, this like fan
kind of type shape here, that where

00:11:44.145 --> 00:11:46.405
it's all coming around that carbon.

00:11:46.455 --> 00:11:48.205
And that's how we can condense it down.

00:11:50.204 --> 00:11:53.755
With that, that's going to move on
to skeletal, which is even easier,

00:11:53.755 --> 00:11:58.425
or geometric, line bond, line
angle, all those different names.

00:11:58.775 --> 00:12:00.865
And what we're going to do is
we're just going to draw each line.

00:12:00.894 --> 00:12:03.305
Remember, every corner represents carbons.

00:12:03.525 --> 00:12:05.714
So we're going to put that double bond in.

00:12:06.375 --> 00:12:08.855
Now we're going to start
showing the zigzag.

00:12:08.855 --> 00:12:11.074
So we have carbon 4, 5,

00:12:13.324 --> 00:12:15.045
and then we do 7 and 8.

00:12:16.345 --> 00:12:21.514
And so being that on carbon number
5, it's currently going downwards.

00:12:21.944 --> 00:12:25.854
So what we're going to do is we're
going to point our t butyl downwards.

00:12:26.494 --> 00:12:29.744
And um, just to kind of answer the
questions, like maybe if we're expanded

00:12:29.764 --> 00:12:34.265
and condensed, It doesn't matter if
you have a pointing downwards or not

00:12:34.295 --> 00:12:36.915
in expanding condensed or upwards.

00:12:36.925 --> 00:12:38.435
It's up to you how you want to do it.

00:12:38.934 --> 00:12:43.234
Um, but this one generally, if a
pretty big rule of thumb when it comes

00:12:43.235 --> 00:12:48.345
to these structures, if you see the
geometric pointing down like this, right?

00:12:48.384 --> 00:12:49.385
Or I have it in like that.

00:12:49.485 --> 00:12:49.705
All right.

00:12:49.715 --> 00:12:51.055
It's actually really
like blue highlighting.

00:12:51.445 --> 00:12:53.235
Let's open this red highlighting here.

00:12:53.635 --> 00:12:55.315
So it's already pointing down.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:59.670
So what we want to do is we want to also
have the branch pointing in the same

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:01.420
direction, which is the thought here.

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:04.480
Okay, let's go take a
look at our next example.

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:10.339
Okay, so for this one, I said, let's
draw one ethyl, two methyl, cyclohexene

00:13:10.439 --> 00:13:12.439
as condensed and as line structure.

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:15.520
So I see a lot of students always
are asking me and how, how do

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:17.069
you draw cyclic structures?

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:20.590
Um, just being that it is
really very, very different.

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:25.770
Um, so the first thing is, is for this one
is let's draw the ring structure first.

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:30.400
So with condensed, what we have to make
sure we do is just like double bonds

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:31.369
is we need to show the connectivity.

00:13:32.739 --> 00:13:35.620
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to draw kind of connecting

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:37.449
all these little double bonds here.

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:40.850
So we're going to start the
double bond is always going

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:42.370
to be number one in this case.

00:13:42.805 --> 00:13:46.255
So when we have this guy, we're
gonna kind of fill that in.

00:13:46.695 --> 00:13:50.355
Now we have an Ethel and a methyl
connected to our double bond.

00:13:50.825 --> 00:13:53.645
So Ethel is at the first
spot, methyl iss at the other.

